rpresser wrote:

 > d3? What, pray tell, is a three-sided die supposed
 > to look like?

There are several implementations.

This one is a stubby cylinder, like a thick coin
(where "edge" is one of the three possible "sides"),
complete with a long explanation for why there must
be a thickness compared to diameter at which it
works:
https://home.comcast.net/~davejanelle/coin3.htm

[The "medium/medial value theorem" for continuous
functions pertains.]

This one looks more like the ball used in US
"football", but not tightly inflated. Compared to
the last one, it has the advantage that it is
symmetrical, and so there is no question about
exactly which parameters make it "fair" in the
physical world. Like the four sided die, the "value"
of a toss is the side on the bottom.
http://www.shodor.org/interactivate/discussions/Polyhedra/

Google image search is your friend.
http://images.google.com/images?q=three.sided.die

It is also perfectly straightforward to just take a
common blank six sided die, and number _two_ of its
sides with each of the values from one to three.

